Coulomb's law can be used to define an electrostatic unit of charge so that the numerical value of the coefficient $k$ in formula (8-2) is 1. **For two equal point charges in vacuum, separated by 1 centimeter and interacting with a force of magnitude 1 dyne ($10^{-5}$ newton), the magnitude of each charge is defined as one absolute electrostatic unit.**
This convention specifies the units of length, force, and charge together;
it does not arbitrarily remove a coefficient from formulas expressed in other systems of units.
